{"duration_api_ms":93935,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"0204548c-a824-57b7-bea3-1783011bc81a","total_cost_usd":0.6614949999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":42,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27040,"cache_read_input_tokens":1211135,"output_tokens":10139,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":865},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27040,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1215,"cache_read_input_tokens":72205,"cache_creation_input_tokens":678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":678},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":42,"outputTokens":10139,"cacheReadInputTokens":1211135,"cacheCreationInputTokens":27040,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.6614949999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":865,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":27040,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":20,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"PKT-0018 is finished and committed (`8efe30d`). The acceptance command `test -s .theseus/oracle/baseline.md` passes, and `theseus validate` reports 0 errors and 1 warning, which I did not look into. The measurement scripts from the attempt that timed out had already been committed by the factory, so this run executed them and recorded the results. No project source was changed. The server-side timestamps come from DocService's DEBUG logs, which were already switched on in the config.\n\n**Where the co-edit delay comes from:** the server is not the cause; the browsers are short of CPU.\n\n| Segment (ms) | 2 clients p50 / p99 | 4 clients p50 / p99 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total round trip | 101 / 294 | 126 / 2 066 |\n| Sender: keystroke → message sent | 46 / 120 | 48 / **1 843** |\n| Server: message received → sent to others | 4 / 69 | 4 / 57 |\n| Receiver: server send → message received | 1 / 198 | 1 / **924** |\n\nThe machine's CPU was about 99% busy throughout, Chromium used up to 76% of it and DocService 3–7%. The server's share stays under 70 ms and doesn't grow with more clients.\n\n**Load test** (lightweight Socket.IO clients instead of browsers, 2 changes/s per document, 30 s per setup):\n- From 10 documents × 5 clients up to 100 × 10 (about 1,770 messages sent out per second), p99 latency stayed around 7–12 ms. DocService used 0.28 of a core.\n- 100 × 10 failed when going through nginx: nginx's default limit of 768 connections tops out at about 700 open connections. I reran it straight against DocService (port 8000).\n- 200 × 5 produced no result within 5 minutes. I didn't find out why.\n- Every save in the load test failed (callback status 3), because the clients replay one recorded edit that doesn't fit each document. Save times under load don't mean much yet.\n\n**Large files:**\n- Converting a file for opening stays at 0.1–0.3 s even for large files, so opening time is spent in the browser.\n- Export to PDF is the expensive step: 1.3–6.5 s.\n- One of three pptx → pdf conversions took 124 s. I can't explain it yet.\n\n**Decisions:**\n- I used browsers plus server log timestamps for the delay breakdown, and lightweight clients for the load test so the load generator wouldn't steal CPU from the server. It used at most 0.14 of a core.\n- I recorded a follow-up request in `.theseus/spawn/` for three things: the 200 × 5 failure, saves that actually succeed under load, and the 124 s outlier.\n\nThe results are a new section in `baseline.md`, the raw numbers are in `.theseus/oracle/perf/results-2026-11-02.json`, and the gaps table is updated. The executor log, the Atlas lessons and a friction entry are filled in. The friction entry says three things: the packet has no time budget even though the first attempt timed out, the acceptance check passes before any work is done, and reusing PKT-0016's setup helped.\n\n**Lessons for the Atlas:**\n- On this 2-CPU machine the co-edit delay comes from the browsers' CPU, not DocsCoServer. Measure the server with lightweight Socket.IO clients, not Chromium.\n- nginx's default connection limit runs out (about 700 connections) long before DocService gets busy.\n- PDF export is the expensive conversion; the conversion done when opening a file stays cheap.","ttft_ms":2206,"type":"result","duration_ms":997696,"uuid":"76e44943-662f-4cb0-ad81-4cdba5c815c8","ttft_stream_ms":1107,"time_to_request_ms":487,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":7,"system_prompt":334,"resume_other":1,"process_user_input":7,"input_attachments":20,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":28,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":10,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":2,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":12,"query_setup":3,"auto_mode_context_wait":6,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":22,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":8,"request_build":14},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":50,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":178,"time_to_request_major_faults":1,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":217,"process_anon_mib_turn_start":141,"process_rss_mib_result":238,"process_anon_mib_result":142,"process_peak_rss_mib":261,"process_cpu_ms_turn":7710,"process_wall_ms_turn":997696,"process_cpu_ms_streaming":1